17307692 267.84)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">16</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"336.43990384615387\" y=\"219.42857142857144\" height=\"28.57142857142857\" width=\"24.280048076923077\" fill=\"#B3B3B3\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"></rect><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(348.5799278846154 267.84)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">23</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(227.1796875 24)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"></text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(227.1796875 301.67999999999995)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Charge (kWh)</text></g></svg><div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for bar graph\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul><li>The data for the 6 categories are as follows:<br><ul><li>0 kilowatt-hours: 6</li><li>8 kilowatt-hours: 1</li><li>9 kilowatt-hours: 2</li><li>11 kilowatt-hours: 4</li><li>16 kilowatt-hours: 1</li><li>23 kilowatt-hours: 1</li></ul></li></ul></div></figure></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">For how many of these <math alttext=\"15\"><mn>15</mn>\n</math> days did the battery receive a charge of <math alttext=\"0 kWh\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>kWh</mtext></math>?</p>","keys":["e61bd15e-594b-4c9b-ad01-fe6ff281b8b2"],"answerOptions":[{"id":"09711208-d18b-4025-920b-40d74c3fa263","content":"<p><math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"757c0bc0-6631-493f-aa6a-78c3f7fb564a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"0098527b-d27f-4057-99dd-5485997ebf76","content":"<p><math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e61bd15e-594b-4c9b-ad01-fe6ff281b8b2","content":"<p><math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is correct. It's given that the bar graph summarizes the charge, in kilowatt-hours <math alttext=\"left parenthesis kWh right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mtext>kWh</mtext></mfenced></math>, a battery received each day for <math alttext=\"15\"><mn>15</mn>\n</math> days. The height of each bar in the bar graph shown represents the number of days the battery received the charge, in <math alttext=\"kWh\"><mtext>kWh</mtext></math>, specified at the bottom of the bar. The bar for a charge of <math alttext=\"0 kWh\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>kWh</mtext></math> reaches a height of <math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math>. Therefore, the battery received a charge of <math alttext=\"0 kWh\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>kWh</mtext></math> for <math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math> of these days.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. This is the charge, in <math alttext=\"kWh\"><mtext>kWh</mtext></math>, that the battery received, not the number of days the battery received this charge.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. This is the number of days the battery received a charge of either <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math>, <math alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn>\n</math>, or <math alttext=\"23\"><mn>23</mn>\n</math> <math alttext=\"kWh\"><mtext>kWh</mtext></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the number of days the battery received a charge of <math alttext=\"11\"><mn>11</mn>\n</math> <math alttext=\"kWh\"><mtext>kWh</mtext></math>.</p>","externalid":"f5598292-13c4-4d60-8b84-b381d3c86f9c","correct_answer":["D"]},"createDate":1743430554988,"updateDate":1743430554988}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430554988, 1743430554988),
    ($SATQ$2a08d878$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$027081-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.E.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Probability and conditional probability$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">There are <span class="italic">n</span>&nbsp;nonfiction books and 12&nbsp;fiction books on a bookshelf. If one of these books is selected at random, what is the probability of selecting a nonfiction book, in terms&nbsp;of&nbsp;<span class="italic">n</span>&nbsp;?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/2a08d878/img_000_9832c30f.png\" alt=\"the fraction n over 12\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/2a08d878/img_001_25236e09.png\" alt=\"the fraction n over n plus 12, end fraction\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/2a08d878/img_002_14617b81.png\" alt=\"the fraction 12 over n\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/2a08d878/img_003_b0126793.png\" alt=\"the fraction 12 over n plus 12, end fraction\"></span></span></p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is correct. Since there are <span class="italic">n</span> nonfiction and 12 fiction books on the bookshelf, <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/2a08d878/img_004_1298a59c.png" alt="n plus 12"></span> represents the total number of books. If one of these books is selected at random, the probability of selecting a nonfiction book is equivalent to the number of nonfiction books divided by the total number of books. Therefore, the probability of selecting a nonfiction book, in terms of <span class="italic">n</span>, is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/2a08d878/img_005_25236e09.png" alt="the fraction with numerator n, and denominator n plus 12, end fraction"></span>.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This expression represents the number of nonfiction books divided by the number of fiction books. Choice C is incorrect. This expression represents the number of fiction books divided by the number of nonfiction books. Choice D is incorrect. This expression represents the probability of selecting a fiction book.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 6, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"2a08d878","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"027081-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.E.","skill_desc":"Probability and conditional probability","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">There are <span class=\"italic\">n</span>&nbsp;nonfiction books and 12&nbsp;fiction books on a bookshelf. If one of these books is selected at random, what is the probability of selecting a nonfiction book, in terms&nbsp;of&nbsp;<span class=\"italic\">n</span>&nbsp;?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/2a08d878/img_000_9832c30f.png\" alt=\"the fraction n over 12\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/2a08d878/img_001_25236e09.png\" alt=\"the fraction n over n plus 12, end fraction\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/2a08d878/img_002_14617b81.png\" alt=\"the fraction 12 over n\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/2a08d878/img_003_b0126793.png\" alt=\"the fraction 12 over n plus 12, end fraction\"></span></span></p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. Since there are <span class=\"italic\">n</span> nonfiction and 12 fiction books on the bookshelf, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/2a08d878/img_004_1298a59c.png\" alt=\"n plus 12\"></span> represents the total number of books. If one of these books is selected at random, the probability of selecting a nonfiction book is equivalent to the number of nonfiction books divided by the total number of books. Therefore, the probability of selecting a nonfiction book, in terms of <span class=\"italic\">n</span>, is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/2a08d878/img_005_25236e09.png\" alt=\"the fraction with numerator n, and denominator n plus 12, end fraction\"></span>.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This expression represents the number of nonfiction books divided by the number of fiction books. Choice C is incorrect. This expression represents the number of fiction books divided by the number of nonfiction books. Choice D is incorrect. This expression represents the probability of selecting a fiction book.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":6,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959621,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"2a08d878","skill_cd":"Q.E.","score_band_range_cd":3,"skill_desc":"Probability and conditional probability","createDate":1691007959621,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"027081-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"9d4f814f-fa61-40dd-869e-e184e740b1d9","difficulty":"E"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"027081-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">There are <span class=\"italic\">n</span>&nbsp;nonfiction books and 12&nbsp;fiction books on a bookshelf. If one of these books is selected at random, what is the probability of selecting a nonfiction book, in terms&nbsp;of&nbsp;<span class=\"italic\">n</span>&nbsp;?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction n over 12\"></span></span></p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction n over n plus 12, end fraction\"></span></span></p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction 12 over n\"></span></span></p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction 12 over n plus 12, end fraction\"></span></span></p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"b","rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. Since there are <span class=\"italic\">n</span> nonfiction and 12 fiction books on the bookshelf, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"n plus 12\"></span> represents the total number of books. If one of these books is selected at random, the probability of selecting a nonfiction book is equivalent to the number of nonfiction books divided by the total number of books. Therefore, the probability of selecting a nonfiction book, in terms of <span class=\"italic\">n</span>, is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction with numerator n, and denominator n plus 12, end fraction\"></span>.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This expression represents the number of nonfiction books divided by the number of fiction books. Choice C is incorrect. This expression represents the number of fiction books divided by the number of nonfiction books. Choice D is incorrect. This expression represents the probability of selecting a fiction book.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959621,"updateDate":1691007959621}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959621, 1691007959621),
    ($SATQ$2a59eb45$SATQ$, $SATQ$b09d507c-3dde-4f24-af93-e07719f875cf$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2f97a175-9a15-4b56-b15a-9983571f0642$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$One-variable data: Distributions and measures of center and spread$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Data set A consists of the heights of <math alttext="75"><mn>75</mn>
</math> buildings and has a mean of <math alttext="32"><mn>32</mn>
</math> meters. Data set B consists of the heights of <math alttext="50"><mn>50</mn>
</math> buildings and has a mean of <math alttext="62"><mn>62</mn>
</math> meters. Data set C consists of the heights of the <math alttext="125"><mn>125</mn>
</math> buildings from data sets A and B. What is the mean, in meters, of data set C?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["44"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["44"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math alttext="44"><mn>44</mn>
</math>. The mean of a data set is computed by dividing the sum of the values in the data set by the number of values in the data set. It's given that data set A consists of the heights of <math alttext="75"><mn>75</mn>
</math> buildings and has a mean of <math alttext="32"><mn>32</mn>
</math> meters. This can be represented by the equation <math alttext="StartFraction x Over 75 EndFraction equals 32"><mrow>
	<mfrac>
			<mi>x</mi>
			<mn>75</mn>
		</mfrac>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>32</mn>
</mrow>
</math>, where <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> represents the sum of the heights of the buildings, in meters, in data set A. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math alttext="75"><mn>75</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="x equals 75 left parenthesis 32 right parenthesis"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>75</mn><mfenced><mn>32</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext="x equals 2,400"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>2,400</mn>
</mrow>
</math> meters. Therefore, the sum of the heights of the buildings in data set A is <math alttext="2,400"><mn>2,400</mn>
</math> meters. It's also given that data set B consists of the heights of <math alttext="50"><mn>50</mn>
</math> buildings and has a mean of <math alttext="62"><mn>62</mn>
</math> meters. This can be represented by the equation <math alttext="StartFraction y Over 50 EndFraction equals 62"><mrow>
	<mfrac>
			<mi>y</mi>
			<mn>50</mn>
		</mfrac>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>62</mn>
</mrow>
</math>, where <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math> represents the sum of the heights of the buildings, in meters, in data set B. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math alttext="50"><mn>50</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="y equals 50 left parenthesis 62 right parenthesis"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn><mfenced><mn>62</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext="y equals 3,100"><mrow>
	<mi>y</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>3,100</mn>
</mrow>
</math> meters. Therefore, the sum of the heights of the buildings in data set B is <math alttext="3,100"><mn>3,100</mn>
</math> meters. Since it's given that data set C consists of the heights of the <math alttext="125"><mn>125</mn>
</math> buildings from data sets A and B, it follows that the mean of data set C is the sum of the heights of the buildings, in meters, in data sets A and B divided by the number of buildings represented in data sets A and B, or <math alttext="StartFraction 2,400 plus 3,100 Over 125 EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2,400</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3,100</mn></mrow><mn>125</mn></mfrac></math>, which is equivalent to <math alttext="44"><mn>44</mn>
</math> meters. Therefore, the mean, in meters, of data set C is <math alttext="44"><mn>44</mn>
</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2a59eb45","external_id":"b09d507c-3dde-4f24-af93-e07719f875cf","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"2f97a175-9a15-4b56-b15a-9983571f0642","uId":"bb791f20-7072-4b03-a56d-34b083361e38","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.C.","skill_desc":"One-variable data: Distributions and measures of center and spread","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Data set A consists of the heights of <math alttext=\"75\"><mn>75</mn>\n</math> buildings and has a mean of <math alttext=\"32\"><mn>32</mn>\n</math> meters. Data set B consists of the heights of <math alttext=\"50\"><mn>50</mn>\n</math> buildings and has a mean of <math alttext=\"62\"><mn>62</mn>\n</math> meters. Data set C consists of the heights of the <math alttext=\"125\"><mn>125</mn>\n</math> buildings from data sets A and B. What is the mean, in meters, of data set C?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["44"],"correct_answer":["44"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"44\"><mn>44</mn>\n</math>. The mean of a data set is computed by dividing the sum of the values in the data set by the number of values in the data set. It's given that data set A consists of the heights of <math alttext=\"75\"><mn>75</mn>\n</math> buildings and has a mean of <math alttext=\"32\"><mn>32</mn>\n</math> meters. This can be represented by the equation <math alttext=\"StartFraction x Over 75 EndFraction equals 32\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t\t<mn>75</mn>\n\t\t</mfrac>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>32</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>, where <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> represents the sum of the heights of the buildings, in meters, in data set A. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"75\"><mn>75</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"x equals 75 left parenthesis 32 right parenthesis\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>75</mn><mfenced><mn>32</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"x equals 2,400\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>2,400</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math> meters. Therefore, the sum of the heights of the buildings in data set A is <math alttext=\"2,400\"><mn>2,400</mn>\n</math> meters. It's also given that data set B consists of the heights of <math alttext=\"50\"><mn>50</mn>\n</math> buildings and has a mean of <math alttext=\"62\"><mn>62</mn>\n</math> meters. This can be represented by the equation <math alttext=\"StartFraction y Over 50 EndFraction equals 62\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t\t\t<mn>50</mn>\n\t\t</mfrac>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>62</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>, where <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math> represents the sum of the heights of the buildings, in meters, in data set B. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"50\"><mn>50</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"y equals 50 left parenthesis 62 right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn><mfenced><mn>62</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"y equals 3,100\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>3,100</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math> meters. Therefore, the sum of the heights of the buildings in data set B is <math alttext=\"3,100\"><mn>3,100</mn>\n</math> meters. Since it's given that data set C consists of the heights of the <math alttext=\"125\"><mn>125</mn>\n</math> buildings from data sets A and B, it follows that the mean of data set C is the sum of the heights of the buildings, in meters, in data sets A and B divided by the number of buildings represented in data sets A and B, or <math alttext=\"StartFraction 2,400 plus 3,100 Over 125 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2,400</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3,100</mn></mrow><mn>125</mn></mfrac></math>, which is equivalent to <math alttext=\"44\"><mn>44</mn>\n</math> meters. Therefore, the mean, in meters, of data set C is <math alttext=\"44\"><mn>44</mn>\n</math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959833,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"2a59eb45","skill_cd":"Q.C.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"One-variable data: Distributions and measures of center and spread","createDate":1691007959833,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":null,"external_id":"b09d507c-3dde-4f24-af93-e07719f875cf","primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"bb791f20-7072-4b03-a56d-34b083361e38","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["44"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"44\"><mn>44</mn>\n</math>. The mean of a data set is computed by dividing the sum of the values in the data set by the number of values in the data set. It's given that data set A consists of the heights of <math alttext=\"75\"><mn>75</mn>\n</math> buildings and has a mean of <math alttext=\"32\"><mn>32</mn>\n</math> meters. This can be represented by the equation <math alttext=\"StartFraction x Over 75 EndFraction equals 32\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t\t<mn>75</mn>\n\t\t</mfrac>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>32</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>, where <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> represents the sum of the heights of the buildings, in meters, in data set A. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"75\"><mn>75</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"x equals 75 left parenthesis 32 right parenthesis\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>75</mn><mfenced><mn>32</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"x equals 2,400\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>2,400</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math> meters. Therefore, the sum of the heights of the buildings in data set A is <math alttext=\"2,400\"><mn>2,400</mn>\n</math> meters. It's also given that data set B consists of the heights of <math alttext=\"50\"><